course: dictionary
lesson: 5107
title: Interrogation: long, close questioning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An interrogation is a long, close questioning of someone to get information. Police officers ask a suspect many careful questions, often in an interrogation room, and check every answer. Interrogate is the verb, and interrogation is the noun.
explain: «The detective led the thief into the interrogation room and asked him where he had been all night.» The words detective, thief and room are clues that this is serious questioning. The questions keep coming because the detective wants the truth.
explain: People also use the word as a joke. «I got a full interrogation when I came home late.» Nobody was locked in a room. A parent just asked lots of questions in a row. You can tell it is a joke from the context. To check your meaning, swap in "a long string of questions": «I got a full long string of questions when I came home late» still makes sense.
fact: An interrogation is long, close questioning, especially by police or soldiers, to get information.
fact: Interrogation is a noun, and it can be countable (interrogations) or uncountable (as in "under interrogation").
fact: «Under interrogation» means being questioned closely to get information.
fact: Humorously, «an interrogation» can mean any long string of questions, such as from a parent.
fact: An interrogative sentence asks a question, and it shares its root with interrogation.
exercise: In the crime show, the detective spent hours asking the suspect question after question. What is this called? | A holiday | A recipe | *An interrogation | A lullaby
exercise: Dad laughs, «Where have you been, who with, why and when? That's a full interrogation!» What does he mean? | A game played with cards | A real police case | A quiet walk in the park | *A long string of questions, as a joke
exercise: «Under interrogation, the thief finally told the truth.» What does under interrogation mean? | Being given a prize | *Being questioned closely to get information | Being sent home early | Being hidden in a box
test: «The interrogation lasted all night, with the same questions asked again and again.» What does interrogation mean here? | A dream | A sports match | *Long, close questioning | A short chat over tea
test: Ella says, «Mum gave me a full interrogation about my homework.» Is Ella joking or describing a police room? | She is describing a story she read | She is feeling ill | *She is joking: a long string of questions from her mum | She is in a police station
exam: In «The interrogation of the witness took three hours», which swap keeps the meaning? | *A long, close questioning | A bright light | A short song | A warm drink
